  "query_preview": "..(This Evening Through Friday)\nIssued at 123 PM MDT Fri Oct 30 2026\n\nA slow-moving deep upper trough is sitting just offshore the Pac NW. \nSouthwesterly flow is stretching across most of the western CONUS \ninto the northern Plains. WV imagery shows a strong shortwave \ncrossing the Great Basin into WY. Surface obs show S/SE winds \ncontinuing with temps in the 40s to lower 50s at this time. Temps \nare struggling to warm across NW SD where low clouds/fog has \nlingered. Webcams show visibility has improved quite a bit but some \nfog is lingering especially across higher ridges and buttes. Low \nclouds will persist tonight with fog/drizzle expected to redevelop \nand expand across large portions of the western SD plains up to the \nfoothills of the Black Hills...and even stretch back into portions \nof the NE WY plains. The fog will likely be dense again over some \nareas through Saturday morning. Dense Fog Advisories may be needed \nagain. Weak ridging ahead of the incoming shortwave will bring some \nwarm air advection in tonight into Saturday. Highs in the 60s to \nnear 70 are expected, but if low clouds/fog end up lingering longer \nthan expected across the western SD plains, this could keep temps \ncooler than forecast. Generally dry weather is expected Saturday, \nthough could see isolated showers across south central SD late Sat \nas the shortwave crosses the region. The Pacific trough/low will \nfinally make its way across the western CONUS through Sunday. \nSurface low pressure will develop across eastern WY through the CO \nFront Range. Southerly return flow will increase ahead of the low \nacross the Neb/SD plains. Highs on Sunday will be in the 60s, but \nthen cooler weather is expected Monday as colder air is ushered in \non the backside of the low as it crosses the northern/central \nPlains. Strong forcing with this system will bring extensive \nrainfall to the region, but models at this time show most of that \nrain falling across East River SD. However, rain is still expected \nacross the CWA...though lower amounts. The exception may be the \nnorthern Black Hills where upslope enhancement could bring higher \ntotals. There still remains much uncertainty as to how much QPF the \narea might see. Gusty NW winds are also expected Monday into Monday \nnight, but again, there is disagreement as to how strong these winds \nmight be. Another variable to consider is how much snow the higher \nBlack Hills might see. This system is quite warm, but light \naccumulations are possible across the highest terrain, depending on \nhow much precip remains as colder air arrives. It's another aspect \nof the storm we'll be watching through the weekend.",
